To generate assert statements based on comments in this C# code, you can use regular expressions to search for comments that start with "@@@". Then, you can replace those comments with a Debug.Assert statement.

Unfortunately, regular expressions can't read the actual C# code context, which means you can't use them to generate relevant assertions. As a result, the following Python code can only match the comments. 

Python Code:

```python
import re

s = """
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);


            int New_Ret = arrList.Count;


            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);


	   // @@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one 
        }
    }
}

"""
comments = re.findall('@@@ .*', s)

for comment in comments:
    print(f'Debug.Assert({re.sub("@@@", "", comment).strip()});')

```

Above code will output:
```csharp
Debug.Assert(count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one);
```
However, this code doesn't generate code to make the assertion - instead it prints the assertion statement. If you want to incorporate this into your C# code, you'll need to manually translate it. It's important to note that this approach is somewhat limited and may not be able to properly generate assertions in complex cases.--------------------Based on the comment "The Count value of the array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one". The C# code statement for asserting this can be as follows:

```cs
