However, to address your understanding, when dealing with the output assertion statements, the "Debug.Assert(condition)" statement can be used to place an assertion in the code which will be used by the assertion verification tools at runtime when conditions are not met. Here's an example:

```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int New_Ret = arrList.Count;
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

	    // @@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "Count value of array is not 0 or greater than 1");
        }
    }
}
```
In this case, the assertion `Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "Count value of array is not 0 or greater than 1");` will be active when the value of `New_Ret` is less than 0. Once the conditions of this assertion do not hold, the "Count value of array is not 0 or greater than 1" will be the message displayed in the assertion.

The idea is to use these assertion statements to provide useful information in case the conditions stated in them do not hold true. In the above example, that may be helpful to understand why the count value of array is not 0 or greater than 1.--------------------// @@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one
